 Blekinge Hospital, Karlskrona, Sweden.\n-   4 Department of Molecular Cell Biology, Institute for Cell Biology, University of Bonn, Bonn, Germany.\n\n-   PMID: **27485408**\n-   PMCID: [PMC5117942](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5117942/)\n-   DOI: [10.1038/ejhg.2016.98](https://doi.org/10.1038/ejhg.2016.98)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nCase Reports\n\n# A new early-onset neuromuscular disorder associated with kyphoscoliosis peptidase (KY) deficiency\n\nCarola Hedberg-Oldfors et al. Eur J Hum Genet. 2016 Dec.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nEur J Hum Genet\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Eur+J+Hum+Genet%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Eur+J+Hum+Genet%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2016 Dec;24(12):1771-1777.\n\ndoi: 10.1038/ejhg.2016.98. Epub 2016 Aug 3.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Carola Hedberg-Oldfors](/?term=Hedberg-Oldfors+C&cauthor_id=27485408)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology and Genetics, Institute of Biomedicine, University of Gothenburg, Gothenburg, Sweden.\") , [Niklas Darin](/?term=Darin+N&cauthor_id=27485408)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Department of Pediatrics, University of Gothenburg, The Queen Silvia Children's Hospital, Gothenburg, Sweden.\") , [Mia Olsson Engman](/?term=Olsson+Engman+M&cauthor_id=27485408)  [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Pediatric Clinic, Blekinge Hospital, Karlskrona, Sweden.\") , [Zacharias Orfanos](/?term=Orfanos+Z&cauthor_id=27485408)  [4](#short-view-affiliation-4 \"Department of Molecular Cell Biology, Institute for Cell Biology, University of Bonn, Bonn, Germany.\") , [Christer Thomsen](/?term=Thomsen+C&cauthor_id=27485408)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology and Genetics, Institute of Biomedicine, University of Gothenburg, Gothenburg, Sweden.\") , [Peter F M van der Ven](/?term=van+der+Ven+PF&cauthor_id=27485408)  [4](#short-view-affiliation-4 \"Department of Molecular Cell Biology, Institute for Cell Biology, University of Bonn, Bonn, Germany.\") , [Anders Oldfors](/?term=Oldfors+A&cauthor_id=27485408)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Department of Pathology and Genetics, Institute of Biomedicine, University of Gothenburg, Gothenburg, Sweden.\")\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Department of Pathology and Genetics, Institute of Biomedicine, University of Gothenburg, Gothenburg, Sweden.\n-   2 Department of Pediatrics, University of Gothenburg, The Queen Silvia Children's Hospital, Gothenburg, Sweden.\n-   3 Pediatric Clinic, Blekinge Hospital, Karlskrona, Sweden.\n-   4 Department of Molecular Cell Biology, Institute for Cell Biology, University of Bonn, Bonn, Germany.\n\n-   PMID: **27485408**\n-   PMCID: [PMC5117942](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5117942/)\n-   DOI: [10.1038/ejhg.2016.98](https://doi.org/10.1038/ejhg.2016.98)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe describe a new early-onset neuromuscular disorder due to a homozygous loss-of-function variant in the kyphoscoliosis peptidase gene (KY). A 7.5-year-old girl with walking difficulties from 2 years of age presented with generalized muscle weakness; mild contractures in the shoulders, hips and feet; cavus feet; and lordosis but no scoliosis. She had previously been operated with Achilles tendon elongation. Whole-body MRI showed atrophy and fatty infiltration in the calf muscles. Biopsy of the vastus lateralis muscle showed variability in fiber size, with some internalized nuclei and numerous very small fibers with variable expression of developmental myosin heavy chain isoforms. Some small fibers showed abnormal sarcomeres with thickened Z-discs and small nemaline rods. Whole-exome sequencing revealed a homozygous one-base deletion (c.1071delG, p.(Thr358Leufs\\*3)) in KY, predicted to result in a truncated protein. Analysis of an RNA panel showed that KY is predominantly expressed in skeletal muscle in humans. A recessive variant in the murine ortholog Ky was previously described in a spontaneously generated mouse mutant with kyphoscoliosis, which developed postnatally and was caused by dystrophy of postural muscles. The abnormal distribution of Xin and Ky-binding partner filamin C in the muscle fibers of our patient was highly similar to their altered localization in ky/ky mouse muscle fibers. We describe the first human case of disease associated with KY inactivation. As in the mouse model, the affected child showed a neuromuscular disorder - but in contrast, no kyphoscoliosis.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/bbe9/5117942/b4793f3745dd/ejhg201698f1.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/bbe9/5117942/3c0f10ecb35e/ejhg201698f1.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nMolecular genetics. ( **a** )…\n\n**\n\nFigure 1\n\n**\n\nMolecular genetics. ( **a** ) Pedigree of the family. Dots indicates confirmed carriers.…\n\n**Figure 1**\n\nMolecular genetics. (**a**) Pedigree of the family. Dots indicates confirmed carriers. (**b**) Chromatogram demonstrating the c.1071delG variant in *KY* in one of the heterozygous parents. The patient was homozygous. (**c**) Tissue expression of *KY* using a human RNA panel screened by RT-PCR, demonstrating that *KY* is mainly expressed in skeletal muscle.\n\n [![Figure 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/bbe9/5117942/f0b712c02d3a/ejhg201698f2.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/bbe9/5117942/f827c90f6180/ejhg201698f2.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nPatient image and whole-body magnetic…\n\n**\n\nFigure 2\n\n**\n\nPatient image and whole-body magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) at 3.0T at 7.5 years…\n\n**Figure 2**\n\nPatient image and whole-body magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) at 3.0T at 7.5 years of age. (**a**) Demonstrating visible muscle atrophy in the lower legs. (**b–f**) Demonstrating that visible muscle atrophy and fat replacement was restricted to the calf muscles with predominant changes on the left side involving the gastrocnemius and soleus muscles, whereas the tibialis anterior and peroneus longus muscles were relatively spared. On the right side, the medial head of the gastrocnemius muscle was predominantly affected. A mild involvement of the vastus intermedius (**c**, **d**) and vastus lateralis (**d**) was also seen. Pasted image, T1, Fast-spin echo (TR/TE=500/8.4 ms). (**c**–**f**) LAVA-FLEX, fat-only phase, (TR/TE=8.4/1.1 and 2.3 ms). Panels **c** and **d** are from different levels of the thighs, while **e** and **f** are from different levels of the lower legs.\n\n [![Figure 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/bbe9/5117942/e636041dfe93/ejhg201698f3.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/bbe9/5117942/92c66f07040f/ejhg201698f3.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nPathology of left vastus lateralis…\n\n**\n\nFigure 3\n\n**\n\nPathology of left vastus lateralis of the quadriceps femoris muscle. ( **a** )…\n\n**Figure 3**\n\nPathology of left vastus lateralis of the quadriceps femoris muscle. (**a**) Variability in muscle fiber size, occasional internalized nuclei and numerous very small fibers (arrows) (hematoxylin and eosin staining). (**b**) The numerous small fibers showed variable myofibrillar ATPase expression (arrows), ATPase, pH 4.3. (**c**) Immunohistochemistry revealed that most small fibers expressed fetal (perinatal) myosin heavy chain. (**d**) Immunohistochemistry revealed that some of the small fibers expressed embryonic (developmental) myosin heavy chain. (**e**, **f**) Some of the small fibers showed abnormal sarcomeres with thickened Z-discs (arrowheads) and rod structures (arrows).\n\n [![Figure 4](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/bbe9/5117942/87596caf3a94/ejhg201698f4.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/bbe9/5117942/7be31f9a998a/ejhg201698f4.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 4\n\n**\n\nSkeletal muscles of the patient…\n\n**\n\nFigure 4\n\n**\n\nSkeletal muscles of the patient show sarcomeric abnormalities similar to those found in *…*\n\n**Figure 4**\n\nSkeletal muscles of the patient show sarcomeric abnormalities similar to those found in *ky/ky* mice. Transverse (left panel) and longitudinal (right panel) sections through a severely affected area of the biopsy stained for FLNC, Xin and desmin. Note colocalization of FLNC and Xin but the absence of desmin enrichment in these areas. The longitudinal section clearly shows that these areas are not amorphous protein aggregates, but filamentous sarcomeric lesions. Scale bar: 10 *μ*m.\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Dickinson AG, Meikle VM: Genetic kyphoscoliosis in mice. Lancet 1973; 1: 1186. - [PubMed](/4123573/)\n2.  1.  Mason RM, Palfrey AJ: Intervertebral disc degeneration in adult mice with hereditary kyphoscoliosis. J Orthop Res 1984; 2: 333–338. - [PubMed](/6527158/)\n3.  1.  Bridges LR, Coulton GR, Howard G, Moss J, Mason RM: The neuromuscular basis of hereditary kyphoscoliosis in the mouse. Muscle Nerve 1992; 15: 172–179. - [PubMed](/1372391/)\n4.  1.  Skynner MJ, Gangadharan U, Coulton GR et al: Genetic mapping of the mouse neuromuscular mutation kyphoscoliosis. Genomics 1995; 25: 207–213. - [PubMed](/7774920/)\n5.  1.  Blanco G, Coulton GR, Biggin A et al: The kyphoscoliosis (ky) mouse is deficient in hypertrophic responses and is caused by a mutation in a novel muscle-specific protein. Hum Mol Genet 2001; 10: 9–16. - [PubMed](/11136708/)\n\nShow all 23 references\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Case Reports\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Case+Reports%22%5Bpt%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Case+Reports)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n-   Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed]